Chapter: EffectWidenScopePort
  cites Net chapter HttpFetch

Section: Body

 scope-admits would accept this. Its portless rule -- "a resource that
 names no port is a resolve-dns hostname, which never reaches a port for
 us to check" -- is true of a RESOURCE and false of a SCOPE: a scope that
 names no port covers every port, which is WIDER than one that names 443,
 not narrower. Reusing scope-admits for subsumption would therefore hand a
 whole host to a parameter granted a single port, which is the kind of
 quiet widening the family split existed to stop in the first place.

 authority-subsumes drops exactly that clause: the outer must name no port
 (covering all) or name the very port the inner names. Here the outer
 names 443 and the inner names none, so it is refused.

  any-port : Text -> [Network.Read "api.example.com", Network.Write "api.example.com"] Result Text Text
  any-port (x) = fetch "https://api.example.com/v1/status"

  apply-443 : (Text -> [Network.Read "api.example.com:443", Network.Write "api.example.com:443"] Result Text Text), Text -> [Network.Read "api.example.com:443", Network.Write "api.example.com:443"] Result Text Text
  apply-443 (f) (x) = f x

  opening : [Network.Read "api.example.com:443", Network.Write "api.example.com:443"] Result Text Text = apply-443 any-port "go"
